<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chalcis</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Chalkida</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chalcis is the chief town of the Greek island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Euboea</a>, on the narrow Euripus strait facing the mainland. In antiquity it was a major city that founded many colonies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, as Negroponte, it was the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> triarchs and of Venetian rule from the 13th century until the Ottoman sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> took it in 1470.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chalcis.jpg" alt="Chalcis" width="320" height="202" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: C messier · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%CE%A7%CE%B1%CE%BB%CE%BA%CE%AF%CE%B4%CE%B1_0067.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Chalcis, also called Chalkida, is the chief city of the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Euboea</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euripus-strait">Euripus Strait</a> at its narrowest point. The name derives from the Greek word for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> or bronze, although there is no trace of any mines in the area. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-middle-ages">Late Middle Ages</a> it was known as Negroponte, an Italian name that came from the Greek phrase meaning to the Euripos, altered by folk etymology so that ponte, bridge, was read as the bridge of Chalcis to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Chalcis is first mentioned in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a>, in the same line as its rival Eretria. In the 8th and 7th centuries BCE its colonists founded thirty townships on the peninsula of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalcidice">Chalcidice</a> and important cities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magna-graecia">Magna Graecia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, among them Naxos, Rhegion, Zankle and Cumae. Pithecusae on the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ischia">Ischia</a>, founded around 770 BCE by Chalcidians and Eretrians, is the first recorded settlement in the West. Chalcidian trade carried its alphabet to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruscans">Etruscans</a> took it up.</p><p>In the late 8th century BCE Chalcis and Eretria fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lelantine-war">Lelantine War</a>, one of the first major wars between Greek cities. Late in the 6th century BCE a war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> broke its prosperity, and the Athenians settled a cleruchy there. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> came to Chalcis in 323 BCE and died there the next year.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> had a trading station in the 12th century and seized the town in 1209, after the Fourth Crusade. Negroponte was shared between Venice and the Veronese barons of the rest of Euboea, known as the triarchs, and became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-christendom">Latin Church</a> diocese. It was attacked by the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a> in 1257–58, by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catalan-company">Catalan Company</a> in 1317 and by the Turks in 1350–51, until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> captured it in 1470 after a long siege. The Ottomans held it against a Venetian attack in 1688.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Chalkida became part of the Greek state after the Greek War of Independence, and a railway link to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> opened in 1904. Surviving sights include the Church of Saint Paraskevi, begun about 1250 as a Dominican priory church, the Roman aqueduct, the Ottoman Karababa Fortress of 1684, and a modern cable-stayed bridge over the strait, opened in 1993.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chalcis” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Garrisons at Corinth, <b>Chalcis</b> and Demetrias, which Philip V reportedly called the ‘fetters of Greece’, anchored Macedonian control of the south.</blockquote><a href="/empire/antigonid-kingdom-of-macedonia">Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Thucydides records that the Corinthian shipwright Ameinocles built four ships for the Samians around 700 BCE, and in the Lelantine War the island is said to have sided with <b>Chalcis</b> while its rival Miletus supported Eretria.</blockquote><a href="/empire/samos">Samos · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Sultan Mehmed II personally led the Ottoman siege of Negroponte (<b>Chalcis</b>), capital of the Venetian possession of Euboea.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-12">12 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>It lost its independence after the Achaean War of 146 BCE, when <b>Chalcis</b> was punished for supporting the Achaeans.</blockquote><a href="/state/euboean-league">Euboean League</a></li></ul></section>
